WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
How can a cell signal with an ion it cannot make or destroy?

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.880
Cyclic AMP can appear, carry information, then disappear.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.520
Calcium atoms remain calcium atoms throughout a response.

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.520
The puzzle is therefore not how to build the messenger.

5
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.680
It is how to change the calcium that proteins can reach.

6
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:28.167
A resting cell keeps free calcium scarce in its cytosol.

7
00:00:29.487 --> 00:00:34.087
Calcium is crowded outside and stored inside the endoplasmic reticulum.

8
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:40.327
Coral pumps maintain that separation by moving calcium away from the cytosol.

9
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:46.987
The result is stored potential: many ions are waiting across membranes, while few

10
00:00:46.987 --> 00:00:49.567
remain free beside cytosolic proteins.

11
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.680
A channel does not push calcium or spend fuel.

12
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.820
It opens a path, and the existing gradient drives calcium through.

13
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.840
Gold channel doors separate, and teal ions enter the cytosol.

14
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.400
The free-calcium gauge rises immediately.

15
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.160
A protein near that opening now encounters calcium that was unavailable a moment

16
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.160
earlier.

17
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:22.680
Now predict. Which machine can raise free cytosolic calcium immediately: opening a

18
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.320
channel, or speeding a pump?

19
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.760
Follow one teal ion.

20
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:33.020
Decide whether each machine brings it toward cytosolic proteins or carries it away.

21
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.200
Ask which direction the calcium moves, not which machine is larger.

22
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:47.317
Opening the channel raises the cytosolic pool.

23
00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:50.097
Speeding the pump lowers it.

24
00:01:51.057 --> 00:01:52.837
That gives two functional teams.

25
00:01:54.277 --> 00:01:58.257
Channels are on mechanisms because they admit calcium to the cytosol.

26
00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:04.737
Pumps, exchangers, and buffers are off mechanisms because they remove calcium from

27
00:02:04.737 --> 00:02:10.897
the free pool. These names describe direction, not molecular shape or energy source.

28
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.560
Calcium signals begin and end by moving calcium, not changing it.

29
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.940
During a rise, entry outruns removal.

30
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.240
When channels close, the off machinery wins again.

31
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.160
Buffers bind some ions quickly.

32
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.640
Pumps return others to the store or outside, restoring the low free concentration

33
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.720
that makes another rise noticeable.

34
00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:39.993
The source can be outside the cell or inside its store.

35
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.913
Either route changes the same measured quantity: free calcium in the cytosol.

36
00:02:47.093 --> 00:02:49.733
Which channel opens determines where the rise begins.

37
00:02:51.093 --> 00:02:54.393
How long it stays open helps determine the rise's timing.

38
00:02:55.793 --> 00:02:59.153
Movement creates a pattern that downstream proteins can read.

39
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:03.577
So the signal is not a newly manufactured substance.

40
00:03:04.757 --> 00:03:08.297
It is a controlled redistribution of an existing ion.

41
00:03:09.537 --> 00:03:11.217
The gradient makes opening fast.

42
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:14.997
The off machinery makes recovery possible.

43
00:03:16.497 --> 00:03:21.197
Together they turn calcium movement into a brief, repeatable change in what

44
00:03:21.197 --> 00:03:23.237
cytosolic proteins can encounter.
